    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                          VETO MESSAGE - No. 64\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 355, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\n      prohibiting the use of drilling fluids, brine, and flowback  water\n      from wells, pools or fields on any highway\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  direct the Department of Environmental Conservation\n(DEC) to prohibit the use of drilling fluids, brine and  flowback  water\nfrom  wells,  pools  or fields on any highway in the State. As stated by\nthe bill sponsors, the purpose of the bill  is  to  prohibit  wastewater\nfrom  hydrofracking  from  being  used on roadways for de-icing or other\npurposes.  The practice of completing oil and gas  production  wells  by\nhydrofracking  has  been  prohibited  in  New  York State since 2015 and\ncurrent state law prevents the issuance of future permits  that  propose\nto use hydrofracking prior to putting a well into production. As such, I\nagree  with  the bill sponsors' interest in ensuring the fluids from the\nhydraulic fracturing process are not applied to State highways.\n \n  While I support the intent to prohibit waste streams  associated  with\nhydrofracking  in  the  State, the bill as written would have unintended\nconsequences as it would ban the road spreading of brine that is entire-\nly unrelated to either hydrofracking or oil and gas production.  Specif-\nically, spreading brine from solution mining wells would  be  prohibited\neven  though  such  wells are producing naturally occurring brine. Like-\nwise, brine produced  in  other  facilities  which  would  otherwise  be\ndisposed of pursuant to a DEC-issued permit would be prohibited.\n \n  As a matter of public safety, state and local governments who maintain\nhighways  apply  materials,  such  as  rock  salt  or brine, in order to\nprepare or respond to weather events. The salt formations  that  produce\nbrine  through  a  well  head  are the same formations mined using other\nphysical extraction methods. While supporters of the bill  have  legiti-\nmate  concerns  about the quality of brine spread on roads, the decision\nto prohibit uses should be based on a technical evaluation of the  mate-\nrial  rather  than  the  source.  DEC  has adopted a regulatory approach\nspecific to brine that submits road spreading brine to  routine  testing\nand  evaluation.  Though  I understand the policy objections inherent in\nthis bill, it would be imprudent to eliminate a tool used for decades by\nlocal governments, thereby burdening such communities and their  taxpay-\ners  with  unexpected costs, based on the assumption that brine presents\nenvironmental concerns rather than a technical evaluation of the materi-\nal in question.\n \n  Instead, further evaluation of DEC's regulatory regime should be taken\nto ensure the correct parameters are being used to monitor brine quality\nand any further regulatory needs should be addressed in DEC's rulemaking\nprocess. To that end, I direct DEC to evaluate the  regulatory  approach\nin  place  and identify whether further rulemaking is necessary based on\nthe profiles of products submitted to DEC to date.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
